  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=4\\sqrt[3]{x}$ at the point $(8,8).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=4\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\sqrt[3]{x}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt[3]{x}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{\u2032}(8)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(8) & =\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt[3]{{8}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt[3]{64}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=\\frac{1}{3}$ and the coordinates of our point $(8,8),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-8 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-8) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{8}{3}+8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
